In article <43ucbr$9ac@news.rwth-aachen.de>,
michaels@mars.stochastik.rwth-aachen.de (Michael Schwingen) writes:

:I got a cheap Dec PC 150 last week (150MHz 21064 EISA board - I think this is
:the 'Jensen" board). After installing the firmware upgrade that came with the
:machine on a disk (now I have 1.0, the  previous  version  claimed it was a
:non-supported prerelease), I am having problems with the ARC console:
:
:When I set the NVRAM default OS to NT, the machine starts the ARC console on
:powerup, tests various devices, and says 'system powerup ok' - and that's all,
:no menu, no choice to run programs or install an OS.
:
:When I switch to the SRM console, I can boot Linux and the machine works  -
:however, I can't run the ECU or firmware upgrade programs from the SRM.

  V1.0 is suitable for NT V3.1, but V1.5 is needed for NT V3.5.
  You'll need Adaptec SCSI ROM revision G (OpenVMS and UNIX require
  later revisions of this ROM).  Firmware part number -- floppies
  for NT and OpenVMS/UNIX -- is QC-01YAA-HC.  (There is firmware for
  various systems at the http://www.digital.com web site.)

  It's not clear if you have a supported graphics controller, and
  an unsupported controller has been known to cause weirdness, and
  particularly at this juncture in the bootstrap process.  (What
  board?  Any LEDs lit on the video board?)  (There could also be a
  conflicting controller setup somewhere: an IRQ collision, etc.)

  When you've >>> SET OS NT, press a <CTRL/P>, press the <halt>
  button, or power-cycle the box.  Select the 'Supplementary Menu'
  from the NT Boot Menu, insert the floppy, select 'Install New
  Firmware', answer the questions, then power cycle the system.
  You may/will now need to 'Reset System to Factory Defaults'.
  Choose `Supplementary Menu/Set Up the System', and then -- with
  the ECU floppy in the drive -- select 'Run ECU Configuration from
  Floppy.'.  You must power-cycle the system.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
  ---------------------------------------------------------------------------




