From: trey@mps.ohio-state.edu (James B. White III)
Subject: DEC f77 vs. f90 vs. cc
Date: 1995/09/25
Message-ID: <trey-2509951245160001@phyntg4.mps.ohio-state.edu>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 110734193
organization: The Ohio State University
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I wrote and executed some simple test programs to compare the
DEC AXP FORTRAN 77, Fortran 90, and C compilers. Here are my
results for those simple codes, along with Linpack and Livermore
Loops benchmarks.

Programs were run on an AlphaServer 2100 4/233 (one processor)
under DEC OSF/1 V3.2B.
Fortran matrix codes are SAXPY form, while C codes are dot-product form.
The codes use the optimized BLAS routines from the Digital
Extended Math Library.

Compile options:

f77 -fast -O5 ... -ldxml
f90 -fast -O5 ... -ldxml
cc -D_INTRINSICS -D_INLINE_INTRINSICS -fp-reorder -O4 ... -ldxml

Measurements are in MFLOPS (bigger is better).

                                f77       f90      cc
Vector Addition (double)

      DO loop                  27.45     16.29    23.00
      BLAS routine (DAXPY)     29.42     23.14    29.68
      Array syntax             *****     28.93    *****
      FORALL loop              *****     16.46    *****

Dot product (double)

      DO loop                  21.86     16.31    21.49
      BLAS routine (DDOT)      22.84     22.05    22.15
      SUM w/array notation     *****     16.21    *****
      DOT_PRODUCT              *****     16.21    *****

Matrix-vector multiplication (double)

      DO loop (no unrolling)   44.69     42.86    23.34
      2-way unrolling          55.91     47.96    35.27
      4-way unrolling          70.91     52.09    46.54
      8-way unrolling          60.03     56.24    35.75
      BLAS routine (DGEMV)     65.00     64.35    56.68
      Array syntax             *****     25.97    *****
      MATMUL                   *****     23.70    *****

LINPACK Benchmark

      Single precision         70.36     63.96    20.63
      Double precision         50.25     43.97    25.22

Livermore Loops Benchmark

      Single precision max    178.04    114.23    *****
      Single precision avg     51.40     43.60    *****
      Single precision min     10.59     10.06    *****

      Double precision max    144.72    109.75    *****
      Double precision avg     42.05     37.46    *****
      Double precision min      9.71     11.36    *****

*** END OF TESTS ***

Some of the results for f90 were confusing and disappointing.
For example, why aren't the intrinsic functions MATMUL and DOT_PRODUCT
almost as fast as the BLAS routines? The optimized BLAS routines were
already around. Why didn't DEC just code MATMUL and DOT_PRODUCT as
wrappers for _DOT and _GEMV?

-- 
James B. White III (Trey)
Theoretical Nuclear Physics Graduate Student
The Ohio State University
trey@mps.ohio-state.edu



