In article <1995Sep26.175254@dax>, wade@dax.asub.arknet.edu writes:
:We have recently upgraded our system here to a VAX 4705.
:(a real screamer for the price!)  My problem is this:
:On the old system I had 2 DHV11-M Q-bus modem control line
:terminal boards.  The new unit will not recognize these when
:they are inserted in the bus.  I thought Q-bus was Q-bus.
:I also have a DecServer 550 that uses a Q-bus style structure.
:
:Does anyone know of any way to get these boards to work in either
:of these units.  These boards ran my modem pool and I really don't 
:want to have to invest in new boards if at all possible.

  Based on experience and the above text, it is likely the CSR and probably
  the interrupt vector settings on one or more Q-bus boards are not correct. 
  When one inserts or removes Q-bus boards, one must check *all* Q-bus
  modules for the correct configuration, as the CSRs and interrupt vectors
  are determined in a cascading fashion.

  In short, one *must* run SYSGEN CONFIGURE, and one *must* specify *all*
  Q-bus modules present, and one *must* determine if all modules are set
  correctly by decoding the module CSR and (when present -- on modules
  that do not use soft-loaded vectors) interrupt vector switchpacks.  And
  one should *never* trust *any* host-based display of the interrupt vector
  setting -- the *only* way to determine the actual interrupt vector setting
  is to pull the board and decode the switchpacks.

  If you omit a Q-bus module from a SYSGEN CONFIGURE, you may end up with
  a non-functional configuration, or a configuration with a missing device,
  or a configuration with a mis-configured device.  What happens depends
  on the CSR setting and the configuration precedence of the omitted module
  among the CSR and precedence settings of all modules present on the Q-bus.

  And no, I don't have the manuals for the various Q-bus boards handy, thus
  I don't know the switchpack CSR encoding scheme used for j-random Q-bus
  module.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
