Hi Daniel,

as you can see, I am also at the RWTH in Aachen. I think I have the board you
are talking about: Highscreen Alpha AXP, costs: 250DM. I also bought the
Adaptec 1742 EISA SCSI-Controller (200DM) and the ELSA Winner 1000 (140DM).

To answer your questions partially:
(See ct't magazine 12/93 Page 130-150)

The Data-Path-Width of the 21064 can be 64 and 128 bit, but I think it is
hardwired to 128 on our board (it does not work with 2 SIMM's).
I think you must have SIMMS with parity.
These two facts are very bad for us of course.

If you buy the board, you should take the 1742EISA for the costs of 200DM
instead of paying 100DM for a 1505ISA. 

The Firmware in the Flash-ROM-Bios does only support:
Adaptec 1742 (SCSI Host-Adapter)
Number Nine GXE (Graphic)      (Basically the sam as Winner 1000)
Compaq Qvision (Graphic)

What I want to say: You may get big big problems trying to boot from a
Hard-Disk that works on a NOT-Suppoted Host-Adapter (f.e. AVA1505).
In the case of the Graphic-Card things seem easyer since booting only
requires VGA-Modes and AlphaLinux will hopefully do the rest (for other Graphic
Adapters).

All I found about AlphaLinux is a WWW homepage:

   http://www.azstarnet.com/~axplinux

Johannes




