mlp@embl-heidelberg.de (Mervi J. Lampinen) wrote:
>In article <1995Sep18.171251.171@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se>, anos@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se (Anders Ostling) writes:
>> Hi
>> 
>> I was at the DECUS meeting in Dublin last week. In order to avoid carrying
>> around two tons worth of paper, I bought the CD that <was supposed> to hold
>> all hand-outs and other seminar materials. 
>> 
>> Not so ! After browsing around, I would estimate that 60% of all files are
>> is missing !!! Did DECUS know about this ? If so, why did they charge the
>> full IEP 25 (approx 50 USD) for the CD. Grrrhhhhh !!!! Arrrgggghhhh !!!!
>> 
>< snip > <snip>
>
>Hi there,
>
>I noticed the same fraud!! Really irritating!! Not only that, but when leaving

I really object to the word 'fraud' here! It makes it sound as though 
there was intention on the part of DECUS Europe to deliberately mislead 
its attendees and this most definitely was not the case.

We never said that all the session notes would be on the CD, and 
attendees have never in the past received all session notes on paper 
either, so to expect a complete set is not realistic.

I also believe that the CD you got was cheaper than if we had provided 
paper copies that you had to purchase. It also had the advantage that all 
the abstracts were there too.

>Decus, attendees were handed a diskette 'Digital Software Strategy' and
>this disk contains V-I-R-U-S-E-S !!!!  Unbelievable !! Is this the 'true'
>Digital Software Strategy to distribute viruses to their client's PCs, I
>wonder ...?

This was really embarassing. Digital produced and delivered these 
floppies without any prior warning to DECUS Europe. And as it was 
Digital, we naturally assumed the floppies were virus free:-(

If you have network access, then you can find the strategy papers and 
more on Digital's WEB site http://www.digital.com/ in PDF format, so no 
viruses there.
>
>Be careful with the diskette, f.ex. SCAN and other sw on the market
>will notice the viruses...

You will need the very latest version of any virus checker to detect 
these. The virus gets triggered when you open a document in Word.

BTW., I was on the Symposium Planning Committee for the Dublin event, 
which was extremely successful with far more attendees than expected.
