On 16 Sep 1995, Leonhard Schneider wrote:

> Could please anybody correct or complete this (probably erroneous)
> matrix I started?

======================================================================
cpu:        ODT     EIS     FIS     FPA     CIS     MMU     I/D
----------------------------------------------------------------------
D11         yes    KEV11   KEV11     -       -       -       -
......................................................................
F11         yes     yes     yes   KEF11AA KEF11BB  KTF11     -
......................................................................
J11         yes     yes     yes    FPJ11     -      yes     yes
......................................................................
T11        option   yes     yes      -       -       -       -
======================================================================

The designations "KDF11-AA" and the like refer to board-level products. 
In the context of this table, I think it's mostly easier to refer to the
processor chips, so I've altered the first column.


KXT11 (T-11 chip) has no HALT instruction and no ODT.  The T11 was
intended as a slave processor or as support processor, and appears in
RQDX controllers, the Single Board Microcomputers (SBC's: Falcon 11/21,
and Falcon-Plus) and DELUA Ethernet controllers.  Some Falcon boards do
have ODT programs (not microcode) in optional diagnostic ROMs (KXT11-A2
option, a pair of 2Kx8 PROMs), though.

EIS (Extended Instruction Set) is standard on most PDP-11s, but a
microcode ROM option (KEV11) for D11 (LSI-11, LSI-11/2, PDP-11/03)
chipsets.  EIS provides integer multiply and divide, and multi-bit shift
operations.

FIS is included in the KEV11 EIS microcode ROM ("microm").  It provides
four instructions for single-precision floating point.

FPA (Floating Point Accelerator) for single- or double-precision
floating point operations.  The microcode ROM option KEF11-AA for KDF11
chipsets requires the MMU to be fitted.  The FPF11 (M8188) option is a
co-processor on a quad-height board for KDF11 processors only; it has a
cable connection to the KEF11-AA socket on the KDF11 board.  The KDJ11
chipset requires the FPJ11 integrated-circuit option (which I think is a
co-processor IC, not just extra microcode and registers) but note that
early KDJ11 boards do NOT work with this option.

CIS (Commercial Instruction Set) provides character- and decimal-string
instructions, primarily as an aid to COBOL programming.  The KEF11-BB
option consists of six chips mounted on a double-width 40-pin carrier.

MMU (Memory Managment Unit) KTF11 is a custom array which contains
memory management registers and logic, and also accumulator registers
for the KEF11-AA (but note that in the very first release, these
registers were inaccessible).  There is no MMU option for D11 or T11
processors; the J11 has an integral MMU.

Hope this helps,

Pete



