In article <1995Sep21.224842.15411@decus.org.au>, geremin@decus.org.au writes:
> I had a call today from a Government Department who are looking for
> "11/93 TURBO boards" for a series of Q-BUS machines with compute 
> intensive tasks (doing image processing and character recognition).
> 
> They already have 11/93 processors runnng at 18MHz and with 4 MB memory.
> 
> They have heard that there is a Turbo board being developed, possibly
> in Europe that will run at twice the speed of the current DEC 11/93.

  I doubt it would be an 11/93-based (J11) board - the fastest I've ever
gotten a J11 to go is about 22Mhz (in a '93). The J11 is a very old design
by today's standards and given the bad blood between Harris and DEC over
it, it's unlikely DEC would contract out any development. DEC could cer-
tainly do an excellent PDP-11 chip design, but I doubt they see any profit
in it.

  Any speed improvements are likely to come from other companies, probably
using semi-custom parts like gate arrays rather than full custom IC's. 
Mentec is certainly a possibility, since they own the product space now.
ROI (the folks who did the 11/93 and the RQZX1) are a possibility as well.
I believe they're from Germany (Rolf Obler Industrieelektronik).

  I have a QED-95 from Quickware. It's a Unibus processor using fast semi-
custom logic, running at about 3X an 11/70. I had heard that they were doing
a Q-bus version some time ago, but I haven't heard anything since. Dropping
a line to ars@quickware.com might get you some info.

