In article <43rrvv$l0k@zeus.london.micrognosis.com>, Waqar Hafiz <whafiz> writes:
:What steps do I need to take if I want to change the node name
:of a VAXServer 3100 running VMS 5.5 ? This node is also on a
:DECNET.

   Standalone node -- no cluster -- and assuming SCSNODE and
   SCSSYSTEMID are in (correct) use:

   $ SET PROC/PRIV=ALL
   $!shut down the net
   $ MC NCP
   NCP> SET EXEC STAT SHUT
   NCP> ^Z
   $! fix SCSSYSTEMID
   $ EDIT SYS$SYSTEM:MODPARAMS.DAT
   make sure that the specified SCSNODE and SCSSYSTEMID
   values match the new/correct values.  (SCSSYSTEMID is
   assigned the value DECnet_area*1024+DECnet_node, and
   SCSNODE matches the new/corrected DECnet node name.)
   If SCSNODE is not currently set, setting it can/will
   change various device names to include the prefix.)
   $! following resets system parameters -- be aware
   $! that this can cause problems for sites that
   $! (incorrectly) SYSGEN-hand-alter parameters
   $! that do not also reset the parameters in the
   $! MODPARAMS.DAT file.)
   $ @SYS$UPDATE:AUTOGEN GETDATA SETPARAMS FEEDBACK
   $! Reset the DECnet databases...
   $ @Sys$Manager:NETCONFIG
   Answer the questions including the new/corrected
   name and/or address; answer "no" to "start the net?"
   $ @Sys$System:SHUTDOWN
   Ask for a reboot.
   
  If the node is in a cluster, you will have to change _both_
  SCSNODE and SCSSYSTEMID, or you will have to reboot the whole
  cluster.  You will also need to update other DECnet databases,
  and/or the local DECdns database if DECdns is in use.

  You *will* want to read the manuals, since I fully expect to
  have left out a couple of steps vital to your specific site.
  (The above is the general procedure, but without knowing the
  details of your site, I can't predict what else -- if anything
  -- needs to be changed.)

