col_eng@Infinet.com (Mike O'Donnell) wrote:
>We have an Infoserver 150 attached to a DELNI using the AUI port.
>Lately, I've noticed that the on the Infoserver, using the "Show
>Ethernet" command, I see a TON of send failures (25,000 errors in an
>hour).  The "send falure reason" value is 00010000, which according to
>the operations guide is a "heartbeat error".
>
[snap]
>
>Any suggestions?
>
>Also, what exactly is the heartbeat for?  I've seen references to it
>relating to thickwire & transceivers, but we don't have any thickwire
>(except the DELNI) so it's not been anything I've had any experience
>with.
>

This is a normal situation, some equipment needs 'heartbeat' and some 
equipment has problems with it.

The 'heartbeat' signal (also called SQE, Signal Quality Enable) is used to
check the transceiver on your network.
This is a digital extention of the ethernet standard.
I assume the DELNI is connected trough a H4005 or H4000.
In local mode the DELNI will generate the heartbeat.
This signal is only available on AUI transceivers.

On a VAX connected to the DELNI you can check the 'heartbeat'
NCP> show known lines count

The counter 'collision detect check failure' has to be 0 with heartbeat 
enabled.
When the heartbeat is disabled the number has to be equal to 'data blocks 
sent'
If this is not so, maybe the transceiver is faulty.








