He takes from my message:

>>In article <43n7ab$imd@usenet.INS.CWRU.Edu>, muswick@v32.uh.cwru.edu (Gary 
James Muswick) writes:

>> Speed is not an issue, cheap and compatible is.  Even in the first example
>> above, speed of the server is not important, function is.

and responds:

>I believe people are not keeping up with the times if they think DEC
>or Tadpole or Conagra could save a lot on price by cutting back on
>speed.  One think which can be saved with slower speeds is battery
>power, but that has minimal effect on purchase price.

>Given that DEC has released so many different models of Alpha so far,
>I find it hard to believe they would support one which was not software
>compatible (within the limits of the proper version of VMS).


By cheap/compatible I meant the following:  That I have a home/portable
(not necessarily laptop) that can run VMS versus MSDOS/WINDOWS/NT, not that
a home/portable/laptop system running VMS would not be compatible with
software on a Alphaserver or Alphastation.

Second, the issue about cost vs. speed is chip price differences between speeds
and the support for them.
A 275 Mhz 21064 is about $700 vs $150 for 66Mhz 21166.  That's $550 plus,
a heftier battery, heat sinking that 200+Mhz in a laptop w/o killing the
support chips and circuit boards all increase the price.  Why is it that we
don't see 133 Mhz Pentium laptops yet?  Because most laptops are using power
saving chips that produce a lot less heat, therefore taking all components in 
the system, you have a cheaper system.  I remember seeing articles concerning
early Pentiums in laptops warping the cases in some instances.  I do not 
think DEC will release a product like that.

My main point was, that there is a market, though small for an Alpha laptop
system.  However, that small market could greatly help server system sales.
Just as the portable Sparc has for Sun.  Hindsight is 20/20, but DEC
management MBA's have done a great job of eroding DEC's market share, because
one division or one product line isn't profitable.

Gary Muswick






