organization: Flying Disk Systems, Inc.
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

I have a suspicion I know the answer to the problem of
reading CD-R disks on an RRD42 (or any other drive).

I did something really stupid (yes, it's confession time)
when I was doing the experiment I described yesterday
(copying a VMS-format CD on a Pinnacle CD-R drive on a PC).
After making the copy, I carefully wrote a label on the disk.
With a ballpoint pen.   That was stupid.   They even tell
you not to do this.   However, maybe someone can learn
from my mistake.

The top of a CD (any kind) is the most sensitive part.
Under the silk-screened artwork is a very thin layer 
"protecting" the gold (or aluminum, for pressed CDs)
reflective layer.   The use of a ballpoint pen will
punch right through this, destroying the gold reflective
layer and leaving the disk full of errors.

My only excuse is that the felt-tip pen I originally
used would simply wipe right off.   What I finally used
(after my big goof) was a Sharpie(tm) Ultra Fine Point
marker (by Sanford(tm)).   It did not rub off.

Would the person who posted the original problem please
carefully examine your CD-R disk and report?   If you
cannot tell if a ballpoint pen was used, look at the
green bottom surface with a magnifying glass for tiny
marks that correspond to writing on the top side.

