In article <43pomc$qoq@news.duke.edu>, 
gallatin@bosco.isds.duke.edu (Andrew Gallatin) writes...
>I'm attempting to move a floppy drive from an AlphaStation 200 4/166
>to an AlphaStation 200 4/100.
> ... but the machine doesn't seem to know the drive is there.

    It's easy to get the floppy cable wrong, so in case that's the problem,
    check the following.  (Note that "Mustang" was the internal code name
    for the AlphaStation 200 series.)

    -- Kenny

    For Mustang
    -----------

    The floppy cable is *not* symmetric!  We've shredded the conductors of
    the ribbon cable so the assembly is easier to route inside the box, in
    addition to the partial cable twist at one end to differentiate drives
    zero and one.  The end which has connector installed on the unshredded,
    intact ribbon goes into the MLB.  The other end has its cable slit to
    reorder the pins.

    Looking at this cable, it appears that Mustang supports only one floppy
    drive.

    The cable plugs into the MLB connector at the edge of the board by the
    power connectors.  The MLB connector is shrouded and keyed with the
    notch facing the middle if the board.  The cable connector is keyed
    with a bump, which faces the middle of the board to match the MLB
    connector.  The colored edge of the ribbon cable (pin 1) will be toward
    the back of the box, right next to the power connectors, if all is
    well.

    The floppy drive that sits right beside the power button
    connects to the connector at the end of the cable.  The
    connector's key (the bumpy thing) goes up.  The colored edge of the
    ribbon cable (pin 1) will be closest to the power connector on the
    floppy drive.

    Ignore the connector in the middle of the cable that looks as though it
    plugs onto a board edge connector.  This is probably for a floppy-based
    tape drive, but you're *much* better off with a SCSI tape.

