Sanford Tong (sanford@connexus.apana.org.au) wrote:

: I work for a manufacturing company, which is currently running a Pentium-66 
: based server, with Novell Netware 3.12. We wish to migrate our main 
: manufacturing/financial/inventory software to the UNIX version, and are 
: currently looking to use an Alpha-based server to support this.

: The number of concurrent users expected to use the UNIX-based software is 
: about 40 maximum, and closer to 25 on average.

: The number of users expected to use other software (DOS based or Windows) 
: is likely to average at about 40-50 (concurrently with the 25-40 UNIX 
: users as specified above).

: From what I have heard, discussions have been conducted between a third 
: party consultant and Digital, and they recommended an ALPHASERVER 4/275 
: system, with 256MB RAM, 12 + 12GB Hard disks (ie. 12GB mirrored), + extra 
: bits. It was also suggested that we run Pathworks, and remove our 
: existing Novell server.

: My questions are:
: 1) Firstly, is 256MB RAM excessive?

It depends on the requirements of the UNIX-based software (could be just 
about anything), but as a very broad rule of thumb I allow about 1 to 2 
MB of memory per user. Thus 40 users equates to 80 MB of memory. So 128 
MB might do the job.

: 2) Given that the majority of users will likely be running Windows/DOS 
: apps, what benefit would there be in removing the existing Novell server? 

Hard to say what benefits there might be in removing resources.

: What's wrong with having the two servers on the same NW? (I imagine 

None that I know of.

: tacking a UNIX box onto an exsiting Novell NW would require far less effort 
: anyway, so what are the benefits (if any) from all the extra work?) 

None that I can see *from your posting*.

: Wouldn't stuffing everything into just one box result in a huge I/O kludge?

Maybe, maybe not. Not enough information to assess this one.

: 3) If you had the 2 servers on the NW, would the choice of server be 
: excessive? (I haven't had hands-on experience with alpha stations, so 
: please comment on how well they perform for this number of users, and 
: this kind of application)

The native NetWare server will probably perform slightly better as a 
NetWare server. The Alpha box is likely to performa better as an 
application server.

: 4) Has anyone had experience with Pathworks? (I don't know exactly what 
: it is - I'm assuming that it's purpose is to support DOS/Windows files at 
: the server - please enlighten me if that's not right)

Pathworks is Digital's PC server software for VAX and Alpha.

: Is Pathworks efficient/slow/barely works/best thing since sliced bread?

Pathworks strength is as an *integration* server, ie. mixing PC's in with 
traditional host-based applications. From what you have said, Pathworks 
sounds like the right functional choice.

Pathworks is not as fast as native (NetWare, etc.) servers, but works 
reasonably quickly, especially on Alpha's. You users might not notice the 
performance difference.

: I would REALLY appreciate any and ALL comments, good or bad. I just wish 
: to have a better idea of how much fact and fiction we are being fed by 
: the consultants, and I know how easy it can be for them to make a fast 
: buck by selling stuff which people don't really need.

I would really have to have much more information than your posting to 
make any firm comments on the situation. There could be a multitude of 
other factors that you have not stated or are not aware of.

From what you have said, the solution looks a little excessive. An 
AlphaServer 1000 (much cheaper), 128 MB memory, and RAID 5 disks (not 
mirrored) might do the job, but then again...more info please.

