>There is really only two major differences between the UNIX on the
>Alpha and pretty much standard BSD.
>
>	1. Byte Order
>	2. Pointers are 64 bits.

Byte order is different from some platforms, but BSD was developed on VAX
processors which have the same byte ordering as the Alpha. PCs are also the
same. Sun and MIPS are not unless it's a Digital MIPS box.

One other, very common problem, is the assumption that a long is 32 bits. I
have to routinely change many variable typed as long to int.

>If your code is trully portable, then you shouldn't have a problem.
>There is only one "ALPHA" hack in our million lines of code.  The
>rest is just to make sure that you really did use htons, etc...
>where appropriate and don't cheat alias pointers to int.

Pretty much true from my experience.

