Steve Kosciuk (kosciuk@bsac.uchc.edu) wrote:
: Hi all,

: I recently acquired some old RA81 and I wanted to make an emergency copy
: of my system disk (also an RA81). I did an image backup of the system 
: disk it seemed to go fine, but when I tried to boot off the new disk
: I got this error:
: %SYSINIT-E- error mounting system device, status = 007280b4
: %SYSINIT-E- error opening or mapping F11BXQP, status = 0018272

: This occurs after the system ( a Vax8200) has joined the cluster 
: and is trying to run sys$system:startup.com. I think the boot block
: might be lost or something, but how would I create a backup that would
: place the boot block in the proper place? 
You can write a bootblock for a 8200 or 11/750 by using writeboot:

$ RUN SYS$SYSTEM:WRITEBOOT
BOOT
1
C000

- But if it reaches as far as joining the cluster it obviously has
found its bootblock, or am I misunderstanding something here?

From the errors all I can say is that it has problems loading the
extended QIO processor.

: Any ideas what I missed???
If you did a backup with physical option it should work. RA disks
maps/translates bad blocks, so I don't think that is the problem.

--
