Subject: Re: MIPS Assembly language question
Date: 1995/09/19
Message-ID: <LINNIG.95Sep19142354@portugal.dseg.ti.com>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 110386419
distribution: na
references: <43ko7h$ff0@news.eecs.umich.edu>
newsgroups: comp.sys.mips,comp.sys.dec,comp.sys.sgi.misc,comp.sys.sgi.hardware

In article <43ko7h$ff0@news.eecs.umich.edu> jlbell@presto.eecs.umich.edu (Jeffrey L. Bell) writes:

> 
> Presently my code looks like this:
> 
> 	lw t1, x
> 	lw t2, y
> 	or t1, t2, t3
> 	sw t3, z
>         ...
> 	.data
> 	x: .word 0
> 	y: .word 0
> 	z: .word 0
> 
> The assembler creates lui instructions for all of the
> loads and stores.  This takes up a good 20% of my executable.
> 


How about using the la instruction to load the base address and using
.struct to create the offsets:


	la s8, data_origin      # load address of data_origin
	...
	lw t1, x(s8)
	lw t2, y(s8)
	or t1, t2, t3
	lw t3, z (s8)
        ...
.data

data_origin:
        .word 0 # x             -- this is the actual storage
        .word 0 # y
        .word 0 # z

.struct	0
	x: .word 0              -- this provides the offsets
	y: .word 0
	z: .word 0




--

