I thought DEC demonstrated an Alpha laptop 3 years ago.  Primary problem
was power.  The 21066 at 66 Mhz would solve that.  It was also before 90+ Mhz
pentiums.

I agree that an Alpha laptop has limited market, and probaly would be hard
for a 3rd party to make money.  DEC can on the other hand, could almost 
give them away and make money.  

Rationale is the following:

1> A friend is a salesperson who works for a company that sells software that
works in a pc-client server system.  The server software runs on various Unix
and also VMS systems. A year ago he picked up a Sparc notebook.  On sales
calls and conferences, he takes his PC and Sparc notebooks, plus overhead
display, and in minutes after arrival, can setup and demonstrate a fully
operational PC windows and Unix Server networked together.  In the past year
he has sold several Sparc Center 2000 systems, Disk farms etc. and a bunch of
PC's.  His software runs on VMS and OSF, but has neither time, cost of 
transporting and setting up Alphastations at remote sites.  Customers buy
what they see.  A Sparc and a PC.  

   This is just one company, there are many like this, where Alphanotebook
visibility would sell big systems.

2> VMS developers and DEC systems software developers who like to spend time
at home.  That are many throw-away VAXstation 2000's in peoples homes running
VMS and Ultrix.  I waited a longtime for an afordable system for home $2-$3K.
When it came, the noname motherboard, it came w/o VMS capability.
I finally bought pentium.  I run Excursion and a 28.8 modem so I can run
my VMS apps / X-win development at home.  A sad way to have to go.  I am
developing more software for the PC environment because I see VMS as dying
slowly and NT moving forward.  DEC has done a good job killing VMS.

   Speed is not an issue, cheap and compitable is.  Even in the first example
above, speed of the server is not important, function is.  Again, though
more developed software, more out in the field systems, will sell bigger
systems for DEC.  Imagine if Emerald had come out 5 years ago with VMS on 386. 
The ease of cluster system management on VMS would be saving coporations alot
of headaches versus the Dos, Windows, 95, NT etc upgrades etc.  It is
something that DEC is hoping NT can do, but they could have owned it.



I wish DEC and other Alpha makers luck.  I see DEC Alpha as the Sony Beta
of the computer industry.  A better system, but you can't convince anyone
of it.


Gary Muswick




