Anders Ostling (anos@elmrd6.ineab.ikea.se) wrote:
: Hi

: I was at the DECUS meeting in Dublin last week. In order to avoid carrying
: around two tons worth of paper, I bought the CD that <was supposed> to hold
: all hand-outs and other seminar materials. 

: Not so ! After browsing around, I would estimate that 60% of all files are
: is missing !!! Did DECUS know about this ? If so, why did they charge the
: full IEP 25 (approx 50 USD) for the CD. Grrrhhhhh !!!! Arrrgggghhhh !!!!

: Somebody from DECUS, please shead some light over this. I am VERY frustrated
: since some of the seminars was of great interest to me and my collegues.

Well, I can't give you a definitive answer but I can probably shed some
light on this. For ten years I was the session notes editor for the Languages
and Tools SIG of US DECUS (until all the volunteers were fired).

During that time we sold books of handout compilations rather than CDs of
scanned images but the general prinicple still holds: "We can only print
what the speaker submits". At the time, I prided myself on getting session
note submission rates of as much as 60-70% of the applicable sessions
(informal or ad hoc sessions, such as Q&A's, were not applicable). Most of
the SIGS did not get that high a submission rate. Many speakers opted for
preparing their session slides a few days before the symposium, which
obviously missed any deadline for getting them published in a book that
was sold at the symposium. They brought a number of handouts with them, or
took business cards and mailed them later, or did nothing at all.

In any event, I doubt whether DECUS ever promised that ALL the sessions would
be on the CD as they know that that is unrealistic. If they have the same
level of spirit and effort that we did in the US before the mass firing, then
I believe they did the best they could to get as many sessions onto the CD as
they could.

Mark Katz
GTE Government Systems



