In article <42khdq$oj2@noc.tor.hookup.net> Paul Rouleau  
<prouleau@montreal.src.ca> writes:
> How stable is DEC Unix cluster? I would like a reference from someone 
> with real life experience with products such as Dec Fail safe.
> 
> Thanks

I met an engineer on the train from DEC here in Switzerland, who was  
saying it was a rushed job under Unix. It certainly is a newer baby than  
clusters under VMS...however...

My experience was working under VMS. Our cluster had two VAX machines (4  
processors each) two VAX machines (two processors) and one alpha (two  
processors).

I was doing finanical analysis for DEC internally here in Geneva (I am a  
contractor). My experience under VMS was very favorable. The best part was  
when I created a code generator for Datatrieve (I know, but it was a  
"Heritage software") and spun off seperate processes running on each of  
the different processors. ( I ran one job for each country,Europe, two  
years =38 seperate processes) Everything worked just fine. (Got my quota  
reduced to only 6 processes at a time -my jobs were the equivalent of  
throwing on some 50 users...)

What you will want to have is some very fast disks for your shared files,  
and for your "power users" Make sure you allow enough space for the  
temporary system files when doing large database processing. This was a  
common problem for me. My disk-bound applications nevertheless ran about  
2.5 times faster on the alpha than on the VAX. Memory-bound applications  
ran about 10X faster. Your mileage may differ...

You will have to recompile your applications for both Alpha and VAX in a  
mixed cluster, but afterwards, it will be transparent to the user.

We had some initial problems when the alpha was added to the cluster, but  
this was rapidly solved, with a minor software patch (January 95, the OS  
was upgraded at the same time to ver 6.2 as well as pathworks... We had  
one major crash during almost a year I was there, but it was due to a  
major power failure, not a computer problem. 

At this point in time you should be able to assume it will be relatively  
smooth sailing. This was my first job working under VMS, and it has been  
the most solid operating system I have ever worked with. If Digital Unix  
has just half the refinement of VMS you will probably be very pleasantly  
surprised...

I personally am looking forward to seeing Openstep Objects/ Apps running  
on top of Digital Unix.  A late-binding runtime system a la NEXTSTEP for  
the GUI combined with DEC's cluster technology, and exploitation of 64 bit  
addressing in Digital Unix makes for the Rolls Royce of systems.
Best regards,
Gerold Rupprecht
Geneva, Switzerland



