Subject: Re: Possible VMS Security Flaws
Date: 1995/09/13
Message-ID: <anonymous-1309951651210001@kawc0o.on.bell.ca>#1/1
X-Deja-AN: 110124141
sender: news@on.bell.ca
x-nntp-posting-host: kawc0o.on.bell.ca
references: <42mta6$ikd@bud.peinet.pe.ca> <42oa2p$pui@news.arc.nasa.gov> <00996196.9403DC40@fcstc1.frco.com>
organization: GEI Inc.
newsgroups: comp.sys.dec

There are no security flaws in VMS.  When VMS is first installed it has a
certain campus flavor but it's up to the system admin people to make
certain security changes based upon local requirements.  If you have
sensitive data that you wish to restrict access to, may have to change
directory/file access bits, owner UICs, or even incorporate ACLs (access
control lists).  It's your responsablility to turn this, not Digital's.

I have been involved in VMS systems admin since 1986 (RSX before that) and
it's always the UNIX guys down the hall who are bitching about breakins
and security violations. Ever read Cliff Stoll's "The Cuckos Egg"? The
whole thing is about UNIX breakins but there is a small bit at the end of
the book that talks about VMS breakins.  It seems that some people where
installing VMS and not changing the default passwords of some system
accounts.  The installation process now forces you to make these changes
and this minor problem has now been fixed (way back in VMS 5.0 I think).
Password history files and forced password changes in VMS have done more 
for security than anything else I can think of.  Happy Computing!



