> I am using QEMM 7.5 (patch #3) with a Digital HiNote CS450 486DX2/50
> notebook computer (recently updated flash BIOS to V1.40).  After much
> tweaking, I have gotten everything optimized with MFT showing 632K
> free!
> 
> I have one strange problem:  The HiNote has a CMOS setting that
> controls the behavior of the power button.  It can be set to "On/off"
> or "Suspend/Resume" and the default is "Suspend/Resume."  I prefer the
> "On/off" setting.
> 
> Here's the problem:  Almost always, after a QEMM Quickboot, the CMOS
> setting goes back to "Suspend/Resume."  I keep setting it back to
> "On/off" and it keeps going back to "Suspend/Resume" after a
> Quickboot.
> 
> If all else fails, I will disable QEMM Quickboot -- but I would prefer
> a better solution.  Can anyone help me? 
> 

Quikboot does it's best to record the state of your system on bootup, then 
restores it to that state and reinvokes DOS in order to speed up the reboot 
process.  It may be that if the default state is suspend/resume and your 
machine must see CMOS run on a reboot to reset it that Quikboot will not get it 
right.  

Unfortunately, some hardware related things like certain SCSI controllers must 
see their configuring utility or CMOS load in order to be recognized properly.  
You can try the bv19 switches and see if they make a difference.  However I 
supspect that be:n may be you only choice if you don't want to manually reset 
the switch each reboot.
