(Dan Gahlinger) writes:

:For more than 10 years now (since 1982) I have known about a flaw
:in the VMS security.  I have contacted DEC about it (several offices 
:local to me) but they didn't seem interested.  I last tested it 
:about three years ago, and found the flaw is still present.  I have 
:also made requests to DEC several times about security enhancements, 
:but they also did not seem interested in those.  Perhaps the 
:security system has changed...
<snip>
:I'll give you an example of what I'm talking about.  In VMS, when 
:you give access to a program, to run it, they need R and E flags at 
:one of the four levels of security...
<snip>
:One hint I can give system admins is a little trick I learned years 
:ago, and that is, you can flag a DIRECTORY as E (without the R) and 
:users can still run and read or write files IF they know the 
:SPECIFIC file spec (complete).
<snip>
:I think it would be morally irresponsible of me to detail any more 
:of the flaws in this medium, but if anyone wants to contact me via 
:email I shall take the request into consideration.  Maybe DEC will 
:see this and ask me about my other ideas... :)

  Interesting -- I was under the impression that the `execute-only'
  access had been documented, supported and intended behaviour for
  quite some time -- I just checked, and this is covered in the Guide
  to OpenVMS System Security manual.  "With a directory file, execute
  access gives you the right to look up files whose names you know."
  (See chapters 4 and 5 for the details.) 

  Security around new version creation and similar was changed at V6.0,
  and now access is generally required to the lower version -- to match
  what is being done to create the higher version.  (One needs write
  access to the lower version to supercede a file -- one is effectively
  overwriting the old version.  With write access to a directory file,
  one can create or delete directory entries.)

  As I have commented in the past, if you believe you have encountered
  a security flaw in OpenVMS, please contact Digital's customer support
  center directly.  Or if you would like to discuss specifics, I can
  forward e-mail directly along to the engineers working on the OpenVMS
  system security components.

