In article <1995Sep7.191036.64@cathy.ijs.si> janez.jaklic@ijs.si (Janez Jaklic, IJS) writes:

>   We are considering a purchase of a DEC 8200 5/300 machine with
>   512 MB - 1 GB memory to serve us as a departmental mini-supercomputer. 
>   Looking at the LINPACK benchmark data, the machine looks pretty 
>   impressive for floating-point computational tasks which we have 
>   in mind. What we fail to see however is how the machine performance
>   would behave when several jobs of few hundred megabytes 
>   would be run concurently.
>
>   The first is the issue of the memory that we can afford. 
>   We don't expect all the jobs to fit in the memory at the same 
>   time, so rather frequent swapping to disk is expected and we 
>   were told that this can be very slow. Is the memory worth 
>   investing into rather than spending money on disks that would 
>   do swaping quicker?
>
>   The second issue is the number of processors. Our programs
>   are not likely to exploit parallelism very much. We would 
>   rather like to see that each job would run on its own processor
>   than switching between jobs which are able to run parallel
>   on several processors at the same time. Is there any experience
>   how the simultaneous access of several processors (running
>   processes in different portions of memory) to the main
>   memory affects performance? What is the real-time speedup for 
>   each of the two single-processor programs which run on a two 
>   processor machine? Is it maybe better to stay with one processor 
>   altogether if one cannot make efficient parallel code?
>
>   Any comments and information will be appreciated.

I can't say this enough, run your most common applications on all the
machines you are serious about (it's usually not more than (2-4). I am
currently using, IBM RS/6000, HP 700 series, and various SGIs. Each
one outperforms the other two on one of my applications by about a
factor of 2. Sometimes the result was predictable, other times it was
a complete surprise.  Multiprocesssor performance is even more
sensative to your application.

Big memory I/O (applications that don't fit into cache) is
a big problem for all machines, it's less of a problem for
the IBM RS/6000. However, the IBM RS/6000 tends to have
slower integer performance than the other vendors, and that
can really be an issue in some codes that you might think
are mostly floating point. All the vendor I've mentioned
(even DEC, even though they orphaned my MIPs box) make machines
that are really fast with some applications. You need to
test to find out which ones are best for your most important
applications. If a vendor can't be bothered to provide a means
for you to do this, what kind of post-sales support can
you expect?

