In article <42oa2p$pui@news.arc.nasa.gov>, wing@tgv.com ("Dan Wing") writes:
|>In article <42mta6$ikd@bud.peinet.pe.ca>, dgahling@cycor.ca (Dan Gahlinger) writes:
|>#For more than 10 years now (since 1982) I have known about a flaw
|>#in the VMS security.  
|>[...]
|>#I'll give you an example of what I'm talking about.  In VMS, when 
|>#you give access to a program, to run it, they need R and E flags at 
|>#one of the four levels of security.  
|>
|>No, a user only needs Execute access to execute a program (.EXE).  A user 
|>needs Read access to execute a DCL .COM file.
|>

Just a mnior nit: if you have E only access to a .COM procedure you can still
execute it. The only difference between E and R access is that with E access
DCL's verification setting is ignored so you cannot see the DCL statements being
echoed as they are executed. (This was introduced somewhere in the V4 timeframe,
1985 or 1986 as I recall).

