A good reason why to look at the 8200 is that the whole design is ready 
for upgrades to much faster processors, i.e. 400 and 500 MHz 21164A early 
next year and even 21264 a year after that.The 256-bit main memory bus 
will only then run at its full 100 MHz (with 300 MHz CPU, using a x4 
clock divisor, it runs only at 75 MHz).

I'd suggest buying one dual-CPU board initially, and taking care that 
there are at least two fully populated memory borads to obtain 4-way 
interleaving for better performance. Next year, you should be able to 
upgrade to quad-CPU 21164A modules if you need more performance. By that 
time, 4 or 8 GB SDRAM memory boards might appear as well. Anyway, 8200 
and 8400 with their 64-bit addressing are the best thing around for 
compute and memory-intensive jobs inside average company & university 
budgets.

Nebojsa Novakovic
PCWorld Singapore
michael@technet.sg

