In article <42mta6$ikd@bud.peinet.pe.ca>, 
dgahling@cycor.ca (Dan Gahlinger) writes:
>For more than 10 years now (since 1982) I have known about a flaw
>in the VMS security.

>I'll give you an example of what I'm talking about.  In VMS, when 
>you give access to a program, to run it, they need R and E flags at 
>one of the four levels of security.  However, this also means they 
>can read, type, copy, rename, and move the file.  This is probably 
>not what you want, it's certainly not acceptable to me.  For some 
>utilities you don't want the user knowing which paths or files are 
>being accessed, and you certainly dont want the user copying those 
>files or moving or renaming them. (by moving or renaming I mean that 
>with the R flag a user can rename the file from the directory it is 
>in to their own home directory - thus moving it).  There's other 
>weird stuff you can do but i've given too much of a hint to the more 
>serious security flaws.
>

Dan --

Much of this no longer applies, if it ever did.  You can execute a program
without having read access to it -- in fact, this is what the E access is for.
(I'm pretty sure this has been true for as long as I've been paying attention,
which is about 10 years; I started on VMS 3.7.)  


Further, the system was changed a while back so that you can't RENAME a
file unless you have Delete access to it -- Read isn't enough any longer.

This seems to fully address the complaints you make above.  Are there others? 
If there genuinely is a current security hole in this area, I'd like to know
what it is.  If you don't feel comfortable discussing it in this forum, we can
take it to private e-mail.

-- Alan

