cannell@pt9530.ped.pto.ford.com (Larry Cannell) writes:
: Scott Collins (scollins@twcnews.twc.com) wrote:
: : However, I would like to warn you, DO NOT USE ADVFS ON A PRODUCTION SYSTEM.
: :                                    ---------------------------------------
: 
: : It is great in concept but it lacks the maturity and tools to manage it 
: : in a production environment.  When the current problems are fixed next
: : year with the release of 4.0,  I think it will be great.  In the mean time,
: : DEC is an excellent choice and had great systems but stick with UFS.
: 
: What problems are you experiencing with advfs? Also, what tools exist for ufs
: that you need for advfs?
: 

While ADVFS works well, any file system that goes down uncleanly needs to
be repaired.  UFS has fsck.  ADVFS uses its file journal to recreate file
structures but there will always be a difference between the data and the
journal if the system goes down abruptly during a write.  What ADVFS lacks
is mature tools to repair the file system when it gets in this condition.
This can have the effect of preventing proper cluster failover or causing 
it to happen incorrectly.  ADVFS does have a utility called msfsck but it's
use and ability to repair problems is limited.  You end up having to back 
up your files and recreate the fileset then restoring.

