In article <42mta6$ikd@bud.peinet.pe.ca>
dgahling@cycor.ca (Dan Gahlinger) wrote:

> For more than 10 years now (since 1982) I have known about a flaw
> in the VMS security.  I have contacted DEC about it (several offices 
> local to me) but they didn't seem interested.  I last tested it 
> about three years ago, and found the flaw is still present.  I have 
> also made requests to DEC several times about security enhancements, 
> but they also did not seem interested in those.  Perhaps the 
> security system has changed, but if VMS still uses the RWED (SGUW) 
> type security, then I'm sure the flaw will remain.  Some portions of 
> the flaws are just annoyances (although serious ones as they can 
> lock the system admin out of the system) and others are security 
> holes that system admins should be taking more seriously.
> 
> I'll give you an example of what I'm talking about.  In VMS, when 
> you give access to a program, to run it, they need R and E flags at 
> one of the four levels of security.  However, this also means they 
> can read, type, copy, rename, and move the file.

I just tried it. A .EXE with only E permission runs fine, but I can't
type it, copy it, or rename it. Renaming has required D permission for
at least five years (since v5?), and probably more.

> This is probably 
> not what you want, it's certainly not acceptable to me.

Why? An example?

> For some 
> utilities you don't want the user knowing which paths or files are 
> being accessed, and you certainly dont want the user copying those 
> files or moving or renaming them. (by moving or renaming I mean that 
> with the R flag a user can rename the file from the directory it is 
> in to their own home directory - thus moving it).

This is completely wrong.

> There's other 
> weird stuff you can do but i've given too much of a hint to the more 
> serious security flaws.

Except they aren't flaws, because what you say isn't correct. Please tell
us some more wierd stuff.

> One hint I can give system admins is a little trick I learned years 
> ago, and that is, you can flag a DIRECTORY as E (without the R) and 
> users can still run and read or write files IF they know the 
> SPECIFIC file spec (complete). Wild cards will not work.

This is exactly what the documentation says should happen, so it's not
really a little trick.

> This way, 
> you can dereference a sensitive EXE by having the users run it, but 
> it runs a different program in the directory or even a completely 
> different path. remember, with the R flag, the user can still copy 
> the EXE so make sure the referenced EXE is not obvious.

But you don't need R permission to run a .EXE.

How do you define a "sensitive" .EXE? One that the users don't know the
name of, it seems? Security by obscurity isn't good security at all,
but it's not OpenVMS's fault when somebody tries to use it.

> I think it would be morally irresponsible of me to detail any more 
> of the flaws in this medium, but if anyone wants to contact me via 
> email I shall take the request into consideration.  Maybe DEC will 
> see this and ask me about my other ideas... :)

Maybe... please post some more. (See disclaimer below.)

Even if what you say above is true, and users can copy .EXE files, how
is that a security flaw?

PJDM
--
Peter Mayne
Digital Equipment Corporation (Australia)
Canberra, ACT
