In article <42mta6$ikd@bud.peinet.pe.ca>, dgahling@cycor.ca (Dan Gahlinger)
 writes:
> I'll give you an example of what I'm talking about.  In VMS, when 
> you give access to a program, to run it, they need R and E flags at 
> one of the four levels of security.  

Wrong.  They need only the E flag.  That's what "E" means.  E for 
execute.  

> However, this also means they 
> can read, type, copy, rename, and move the file.  

Read, type, and copy, yes.  (Who cares?  If they can run it, they are
"copying" it into their own address space.)  But renaming and
"moving", as you call it (which is just renaming to a different
directory) require DELETE access to the file.  This is a fairly recent
(5.0?) change. 

Renaming (or deleting) the file also requires write access to the
directory the file is in, and this has ALWAYS been the case... In fact
it was that way on ODS-1 under RSX-11M! 

> One hint I can give system admins is a little trick I learned years 
> ago, and that is, you can flag a DIRECTORY as E (without the R) and 
> users can still run and read or write files IF they know the 
> SPECIFIC file spec (complete). 

This was implemented and documented "years ago".  (3.x, I believe.) 
What's the big revelation here? 

> I have been a long time DEC PDP and VAX user (since 1976) and have 
> always loved the VMS operating system.  I have spent several years 
> at the administrative level of VAX operations (on 730, 750, 780, 
> 782, 8600, MicroVax II, and MicroVax 2000).

Then maybe you should learn a little more about it?  No wonder DEC 
paid no attention to you -- based on what you've written here, you've 
no idea what you're talking about.  
 
(p.s.:  On VMS we call them "system managers", not "admins".)

