richardp@iinet.com.au (Richard Paton) wrote:

>I'm new to VME stuff, but need to write a device driver that accesses 
>shared memory on the VME bus (we've got a comms card that can share its 
>memory on the bus).  I've been told that the only way that the Alpha 
>processor can access this memory-on-the-bus is by DMA.  Is this true? or 
>can the alpha access this shared memory as if it were local.

>If DMA is the only method, what other methods are available to 
>communicate with devices on the VME bus?

Whether or not the Alpha can access the bus depends mainly on the card
design.  I'd be extremely surprised if it could not, since that would
make it almost impossible to use.  I don't know how the Alpha treats
the world - can it address by bytes, or is everything a longword (eg.,
address 1 is the first longword, address 2 isn't the second byte, but
the second longword)?  Some DSPs do this and it makes use of the
VMEbus awkward.  

Check the user manual for the Alpha card; it should specify the
processor's memory space map, and accesses in a certain range will be
directed out to the bus.  If the processor cannot access bytes or
words, I would expect that to be clearly noted in that area of the
manual.

Let the newsgroup know which Alpha card you're using, and someone may
have some familiarity with it...

-Andy


