In article <42fttb$jum@ultrix.FOUR.net>, chrissy@FOUR.net (System Administrator) writes:

>I have a Personal DECstation 5000 Model 25 which currently has a
>DEC RZ57 (1.2Gb) drive on it's SCSI bus.  I would like to install
>a larger capacity drive, possibly a 4Gb DEC 5400S.
>
>Can the machine support drives larger than 1.2Gb?

Yes.  The SCSI driver for ULTRIX V4.1 and earlier had a 1 GB
limit, but I'm pretty sure that the Personal DECstation needs
a version or two after V4.1 so that won't be problem.  V4.4
does support the RZ74.  Earlier versions either had latent
support for large disks or relied on SCSI devices to behave
reasonably.

ULTRIX has a 2 GB partition size limit, so you'll have to
repartition a large disk.  Create a small file system on
the A partition (this writes the partition table to the
disk), then repartition to taste.  Create file systems
as appropriate.

>Does anyone know if the machine has a SCSI-I or SCSI-II?

I think the Personal DECstation was designed before the SCSI-2
specificiation became a standard, but it was pretty solid by
then.  The SCSI adapter is probably single ended, narrow and
slow.  It does support synchronous transfers.  It may support
tagged command queing, but I wouldn't be brave enough to find
out...

