From: Wiegel@wpo.sosc.osshe.edu (Ron Wiegel)

>I'm in the process of looking for new tape drives for
>my DEC Alpha.  I'm currently using TZ86-TS's, and am 
>thinking of moving to TZ87-TA's.  In researching the 
>TZ87 drives, I have run across DLT2000XT (Quantum)
>tape drives. According to the vendor they are
>the same tape drive as the TZ87, at less than half
>the price. 

>Has any one used or researched the DLT2000XT tape
>drives?  If so would you purchase them?  Are they 
>the same as the TX87-TA, with a different label?
>Are there any known problems?

We went through the same process last year.  We bought
DLT2700 at a 45% discount DEC's best price (corporate discount)
over the TZ877.

When I spoke to DEC magnetics division (just before Quantum
bought it), I was told that the drives were 100% identical
except for the tape loadable firmware.  They said if I had
compatibility problems, I could a TZ87 firmware tape from
DEC.  We have been field testing HSM 1.1 and 1.2, which does
not currently support the DLT2700, but does support the
TZ877.  When we discovered all sorts of problems using the
loader, I inquired to DEC and Quantum about the TZ87 firmware
tape.  I then got a different response.

It seems that the drives are physically the same except that
besides the firmware software, there is a chip that identifies
the drive as a DLT2700 or TZ87 or whatever, so that only that
firmware tape for that device can be loaded.   Supposedly you
can't change the ID chip.  Quantum does provide a utility that
runs on a PC with a SCSI bus (argh!!!) called DLT tools that
will allow you to change the Vendor ID and Model ID of the 
drive.  We changed it to be DEC, TZ87.  Interestingly, the
when we plugged it into our Alpha 2100, VMS reports a TZ877.
It seems to function just fine.  Though tape handling within
HSM seems a bit slow.

According to Quantum the only difference between the TZ87 and
DLT2000, is the type of information returned for diagnostics.
How important that is, I don't know.  But DEC charges a heck
of alot of money (45%) premium, for that firmware.

My advice is that, if you are someone that would buy an Exabyte
drive and use it, (versus a DEC TZK08 or TZK09 etc. 8mm) than the
DLT2000 or DLT4000 (40GB compressed) is a great deal.  If you
are conservative in your hardware approach, buy the TZ87 (esp.
after DEC announces the TZ88.)

Good Luck
Gary Muswick
Image Processing Engineer
University Hospitals of Cleveland.



   
   



