In article <42iaqu$bna@aplinfo.jhuapl.edu>, humesdg1@aplcomm.jhuapl.edu (David G. Humes) writes:
>Greetings,
>
>We are considering the purchase of three DEC Alpha 2100 servers.  It's 
>likely that they will all run Digital UNIX, but one may run VMS.  We have a 
>requirement to read and write data to and from Exabyte 8mm tape drives on 
>these servers.  Although the DEC salesman has said that it's no problem
>with Digital UNIX, we're no so certain about VMS.  So, could someone who 
>actually is using these things comment on whether Exabyte 8mm drives are 
>supported under both VMS and Digital UNIX.  Also, since there are now several 
>models of the Exabyte (8200, 8500, 8505), please remark on which models are 
>supported.
>

We use 'em.  At least 8200 and 8500.  According to statements which DEC ships
with it's 8mm drives, VMS supports 8mm only for the purposes of backup.  The
VMS BACKUP utility indeed works correctly with them.

There are some operations which are known not to work from VMS on an 8mm tape.
Primarily using RMS to create the first file on an ANSI/ASCII labeled tape. 
A workaround is to use BACKUP to create a dummy file since BACKUP creates
ANSI/ASCII labeled tapes.

Nowdays there's a very good chance that you won't be working with ANSI/ASCII
lables anyhow.

Many of the tape handling utilities for non ANSI/ASCII labeled tapes do not use
RMS.  For example, we got a tar utility off the net which uses $QIOWs.  You can
easily write your own utilities to do tape I/O via the $QIO[W] system service,
so your own code doesn't have to go through RMS.

You can also use the POSIX kit to load supported versions of tar et. al.  Since
these go through the POSIX path to the kernel, they're not using RMS either.

So the official support is for BACKUP only, but in real life they do what you
buy them for.  We know of more than one project which relies on 8mm on VMS
systems for mission critical operations.

