In article <42g1hm$c4k_001@news.fast.net>, kshuff@fast.net (kshuff) writes:
|> This is another plea for help with a VAX 11/750. The problem is with the 
|> console terminal, it will receive data but it will not transmit. Is this a 
|> hardware problem or is it in the code itself. All the remote terminals work 
|> fine. What physically drives the console, I was told it was the TU58 board but 
|> I cannot find any serial devices that drive the console. Any and all help 
|> would greatly be appreciated. Thanks
|> 
I had an intermittent problem similar to this when I first got my 750--
occasionally, the console terminal would stop producing output. This turned
out to be a bad solder joint on one of the backplane power connectors 
on the CPU. The connectors in question are (9-way, I think) at the top 
of the rear side of the backplane. Try wiggling the cable harness that connects
to the connector and see if anything happens -- that's how I diagnosed 
the problem on my machine. You could also try connecting one of those
little light-box RS232 monitors between the terminal and the serial cable,
and see if the drivers are producing any output at all. I found that
the fault on mine caused the output to appear floating (i.e. neither 
LED for the data output line lit). Re-flowing the solder on the dodgy
connection cured the problem. 

If you need to replace the line-drivers, they're on the UBI board 
and they're the usual parts (1488 or 1489, can't remember which is which).

James
