It is slightly more complicated than this.

The Digital variants of these Exabyte drives have Digital specific
firmware on them.  The firmware may only be different in that the devices
will show up with a SCSI ID that includes the Digital part name, or there
may be other functional differences as well.

In any case, some 8MM drives have capabilities to write data in different
densities and/or with data compression.  The VMS SCSI magtape driver
(MKDRIVER) ONLY knows how to command the Digital variants of these drivers
to operate in the different density or compaction modes, and when using
generic Exabyte drives will NOT issue any commands to control the media
density/compaction. This ends up meaning that you can usually READ (an
most likely rewrite) any tape that was written at any density the drive is
capable of reading, but you will only be able to WRITE at the default
drive density for the drive your've got.

Actually, the MKDRIVER doesn't even always know how to command the drive
write density to all Digital drives.  For example, I've got a TZ87 on my
AXP system running OpenVMS V6.1, and I've applied all the available SCSI
patches, but all efforts at specifying media compaction programatically
have no effect.  The only good news here is that this particular drive has
an external set of buttons to manually select the desired density, and
this does work.

