humesdg1@aplcomm.jhuapl.edu (David G. Humes) wrote:
>Greetings,
>
>We are considering the purchase of three DEC Alpha 2100 servers.  It's 
>likely that they will all run Digital UNIX, but one may run VMS.  We have a 
>requirement to read and write data to and from Exabyte 8mm tape drives on 
>these servers.  Although the DEC salesman has said that it's no problem
>with Digital UNIX, we're no so certain about VMS.  So, could someone who 
>actually is using these things comment on whether Exabyte 8mm drives are 
>supported under both VMS and Digital UNIX.  Also, since there are now several 
>models of the Exabyte (8200, 8500, 8505), please remark on which models are 
>supported.
>
>Thanks in advance.
>
>--Dave Humes
>
>===========================================================================
>Dave Humes                                >> humesdg1@aplcomm.jhuapl.edu <<
>===========================================================================
>
>

The EXABYTE 8mm hardware has a limitation that is not seen in other types of tape 
drives. It is unable to WRITE APPEND. (It is unable to append data to existing data 
blocks.) You do not normally encounter this problem, except when creating the first 
file on an ANSI labeled tape. (Then a HDR record must be written following an 
existing VOL record.)  Newer versions of VMS have added code to MKDRIVER to work 
around this EXABYTE hardware problem. In many cases it is now possible to create 
ANSI labeled tapes, but under some conditions it still fails. The cases for which we 
found it still fails are:

1) The tape was initialized with the /PROTECTION qualifier in the INITIALIZE 
command. 

or,

2) The tape is served to the cluster (a new feature of VMS 6.2) and the node to 
which the drive is physically connected is not the one on which the program runs 
that creates this first file. 

We complained about this to Digital, but their response was that EXABYTE drives are 
not officially supported.

