 ichihara@rikvax.riken.go.jp (Takashi Ichihara <ichihara@rikvax.riken.go.jp, 41910: :ichihara>) writes:
>   Hi
>
>   We were using 20GB RAID5 disk (with CMD SCSI RAID Controller:
>CRD-5000) on the Open VMS AXP V6.1 (DEC 3000-400) and it was of no 
>problem when the VMS was 6.1. Also the Segate ST410800N disks (9GB) 
>were of no problem.  Everything was fine when it was VMS V6.1.
>
>   The problem occurred after upgrading the VMS to V6.2.  In Open VMS
>AXP V6.2, this RAID5 disk (CMD) cannot be used at all..  For example, 
>DCL "initialize" command on this disk results "medium is offline" 
>while the disk is really on-line.   When we switched back to the 
>VMS V6.1 then there is no problem in this raid5 disk.   
[snip]

Takashi,

Glad I can help out, since I just went through and solved this problem!

After several discussions with DEC Engineering and CMD Tech Support I can
summarize as follows:

The OpenVMS 6.2 SCSI driver now supports tagged command queuing.
Unfortunately, the DEC implementation is incompatible (and possibly
flawed) with the standard CMD implementation.  Fortunately, 
you have two choices:

   1)  Turn off tagged command queueing in the host channel parameters
       of the CRD-5000.  Then restart the RAID controller and reboot
       your system.  This should cause the DEC driver to not attempt
       to use the feature and everything should work fine. (But I've
       only tested this on firmare D3B).

   2)  CMD has an interim firmware upgrade which will fully support
       tagged command queueing.  You can download it from their
       BBS: (714) 454-0795.   The file you need is "CRD_D3C.ZIP"
       but since this is an "unsupported" release it won't show up
       in their directory.  You just need to enter the name and
       download it.

I can vouch that both of these fixes will work.  If you need more help 
you can contact me, or contact Todd Graham at CMD who is very helpful 
(graham@cmd.com).  DEC Engineering is also working on the problem.

>  Or is it a time to say,
> "Farawell to VMS" ?     Thanks for any information.
                                                      
Don't give up on VMS -- its still the best OS around...

-- Randy Rushe




