In article <1995Aug29.051818.15373@decus.org.au>, manson@decus.org.au writes:

> In article <41oufj$s2s@news.ios.com>, Dave Kneitel <dkneit@soho.ios.com> writes:
>> aarons@ihs.com (Aaron Sliwinski) wrote:
>>>
>>> Does anyone out there know the wiring of the original MicroVAX II
>>> console cable?  What I am after specifically is which pins are
>>> connected together on the 9-pin (CPU) end.  

DEC had fun with these connectors.  Pin 1 is ground, Pins 2&3 are
receive and transmit (not sure which is which).  The important thing
is that grounding Pin 8 enables "console mode", which will ignore the
video card on a uVAX II or VAXstation or other such critter. An
important thing to know also is that Pin (I think) 7 has 12V available
on it.  So you don't want to go indiscriminately connecting this to
something.  The printer serial cable is different and does not have
Pin 8 grounded.

So to make a console cable, connect Pins 1 (gnd), 8 (console), and 9
(shield) together on the uVAX end, and run Pins 1, 2, and 3 to your
terminal. 

I had good luck soldering a male and female DB9 together with a piece
of wire wrapped around the approprate pins.  This allows you to use
standard 9-pin serial cables.

this should work for you,

  - Pavl Zachary (using this account)



