From: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
Subject: Re: VMS V6.2 SCSI Disk Driver Problem
Date: 1995/09/04
Message-ID: <42fbat$4ft@nntpd.lkg.dec.com>
X-Deja-AN: 109471693
distribution: world
references: <1995Sep2.180145.28642@riksun.riken.go.jp>
organization: Digital Equipment Corp
reply-to: hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com (Stephen Hoffman)
newsgroups: comp.os.vms,comp.sys.dec,vmsnet.alpha,vmsnet.misc,comp.periphs.scsi



    Most of the changes made to the SCSI interface were made in
    support SCSI clusters -- to enable access to SCSI features
    such as tagged command queuing.

In article <1995Sep2.180145.28642@riksun.riken.go.jp>,
ichihara@rikvax.riken.go.jp (Takashi Ichihara <ichihara@rikvax.riken.go.jp,
41910: :ichihara>) writes:

:   We were using 20GB RAID5 disk (with CMD SCSI RAID Controller:
:CRD-5000) on the Open VMS AXP V6.1 (DEC 3000-400) and it was of no 
:problem when the VMS was 6.1. Also the Segate ST410800N disks (9GB) 
:were of no problem.  Everything was fine when it was VMS V6.1.
:
:   The problem occurred after upgrading the VMS to V6.2.  In Open VMS
:AXP V6.2, this RAID5 disk (CMD) cannot be used at all..  For example, 
:DCL "initialize" command on this disk results "medium is offline" 
:while the disk is really on-line.   When we switched back to the 
:VMS V6.1 then there is no problem in this raid5 disk.   
:
:  The malfunction of the third-party RAID5 disk (with CMD CRD-5000 
:controller) seems to be due to the *crazy* SCSI disk driver in 
:VMS V6.2 as written in the Open VMS V6.2 release note:
:
:   4.11.2.3 SCSI Port and Class Drivers
:      4.11.2.3.1 Mounting Third-party SCSI Disks
: 
:  [VMS V6.2 issues a 10-byte mode SCSI sense command (which is not 
:   supported in major vendors in the world) instead of a standard 
:   6-byte mode sense command.  .....  ]

   These drives do not appear to be complying with the SCSI-2 standard
   around the MODE_SENSE(10) command.  These drives should either reject
   the MODE_SENSE(10) commands per the SCSI-2 standard, or they should
   support the commands -- this processing is per the SCSI-2 standard. 

   That OpenVMS is one of the few operating systems that is using the
   MODE_SENSE(10) (and is then falling back to the MODE_SENSE(6)) is
   one thing -- that the drives are not returning either the correct
   results or are returning the "not supported" sequence is another.

   Most SCSI-2 devices correctly perform one of these two sequences, and
   (as far as I know) all SCSI-2 Digital SCSI disk drives process the
   MODE_SENSE(10) command per the SCSI-2 standards.

   However, it's likely that the CMD drive is not returning a required
   MODE_PAGE, and was never returning one -- prior to OpenVMS V6.2, the
   SCSI driver issued a MODE_SENSE request of "Return all pages", and
   the pre-V6.2 SCSI support included a mistake (bug) that allowed this
   particular device online with some default values.

:   Also we have observed 11 error-counts for all the Segate ST410800N
:9GB disks (rev. 0019) during the configure procedure before mounting
:disks. (Error log says "unknown SCSI command", but this is not a 
:serious problem.)  The serious problem for us is that the CMD RAID5 
:disk does not work at all in VMS 6.2)

    This is documented in the OpenVMS V6.2 release notes, section
    4.11.2.3.  It states that "errors logged against these disks
    do not indicate a real problem".  OpenVMS has detected an error
    in the MODE_SENSE(10) processing, and has fallen back to the
    MODE_SENSE(6) processing, though the failure is (rather obviously)
    logged.  (This is the best of a bad situation -- OpenVMS can be
    quiet about the error, or it can log it...)

    You might check with Seagate here.

:   Does anyone know when the patch will be released for this *crazy* 
:SCSI disk driver of the Open VMS AXP V6.2 ?  Or is it a time to say,
:"Farawell to VMS" ?     Thanks for any information.

    To see what is happening with the SCSI drives, issue the command

	$ MCR SYS$ETC:SCSI_INFO <device>:

    The driver will ask for each "required", "desired" and "optional"
    page, one page one at a time.  If a MODE_PAGE attribute on a
    "required" page is not available, an errorlog entry will be
    written and the device can not and will not be mounted.

    I believe CMD may have a firmware update available as a fix (or
    as a workaround) for the "MODE_PAGE" situation.  I do not know
    if (or when) there will be a patch available for OpenVMS.

    You might check with CMD for the update.

  ------------------------------ Opinionative -------------------------------
   Stephen Hoffman      OpenVMS Engineering      hoffman@xdelta.enet.dec.com
